## Comparison and Discussion

In the recorded run Prim is faster on the small case, while Kruskal is faster on medium and large cases. Lazy Prim scans 2E entries and empties the heap, including candidates discarded after their destination is visited. Kruskal sorts all E edges but can stop accepting/examining edges once V−1 are chosen. For V=1000, Kruskal examines 2753 sorted edges and makes 50410 sort comparisons; Prim examines 10000 adjacency entries and makes 111014 heap comparisons. This helps explain the observed ordering but does not establish universal superiority. Graph density, implementation constants, early stopping and runtime noise matter.

SCC examines exactly 3E edges: original DFS, transpose construction, transpose DFS. Increasing (V,E) from (100,500) to (1000,5000) increases both sizes tenfold; runtime grows roughly twelvefold in this run. This is compatible with O(V+E), without proving asymptotic behavior from three measurements. Small cases have substantial fixed overhead.

DAG topology examines 2E edges, while the fully cyclic family examines E during indegree counting and processes zero vertices. Its smaller runtime does not mean cyclic ordering succeeds; it detects impossibility earlier. This family does not exercise cycles hidden behind a long acyclic prefix; correctness tests cover other structures.

Dijkstra examines E edges for validation and E during traversal because every benchmark vertex is reachable. Its heap work increases with successful relaxations, rather than only V: at V=1000 there are 1440 pushes and pops. Time generally grows with graph size but varies due to the JVM and queue behavior. Operation counts describe work more consistently than these short timings. The report does not extrapolate a reliable performance ratio beyond the tested cases.

## Testing

Run `sh run.sh test`. The test runner throws explicit assertions, so it does not require `-ea`.

Tests cover known small MSTs, disconnected forests, cycles, equal weights/multiple valid MSTs, negative MST weights, zero-weight paths, unreachable vertices, a single vertex, self-loops, parallel edges, DSU connectivity and negative-edge rejection by Dijkstra.

Independent checks include exhaustive spanning-tree enumeration for 25 random complete five-vertex graphs, Bellman–Ford reference distances and actual path costs for 100 random directed graphs from every source, and Floyd–Warshall reachability for exact SCC membership. Every vertex must occur exactly once in an SCC; two vertices share a component exactly when mutually reachable. DAG tests verify uniqueness, coverage and every edge's order. The benchmark checks agreement of Kruskal and Prim for each required graph size. Recorded test output: **PASS: 33296 correctness checks**.

## Conclusion

Use MST algorithms to minimize the total cost of an undirected connecting network, SCC to identify mutually reachable directed regions, topological ordering to schedule acyclic dependencies, and Dijkstra to obtain shortest routes with non-negative weights. An MST does not minimize individual source-to-destination route lengths. Kruskal and Prim agree on total optimal cost; their actual edge selections can differ under ties. The measured trends are consistent with sorting/heap costs versus linear graph traversal, within the limitations of five short JVM measurements.

